1.	Please summarize the following server components: 
 Motherboard (System Board)
The motherboard is the main circuit board of the server.
 It connects the CPU, RAM, storage, network cards, and other components, and provides power and data paths so all parts can communicate with each other.

CPU (Central Processing Unit)
The CPU is the “brain” of the server. It executes instructions from the operating system and applications and performs all the main calculations and logical operations.

RAM (Memory)
RAM is fast, temporary memory. It stores the data and code that the CPU needs while programs are running. Its contents are lost when the server is powered off.

Storage Drives (HDD/SSD/NVMe)
Storage drives provide permanent storage for the operating system, applications, logs, and data.
* HDDs are mechanical and usually slower but cheaper.
* SSDs and NVMe drives use flash memory and offer much higher read/write speeds, which is useful for heavy workloads.

RAID Controller (Smart Array)
The RAID controller manages multiple physical drives as a single logical unit. It can be configured to improve performance, increase redundancy, or both, using RAID levels such as RAID 1, RAID 5, or RAID 10.

Power Supply Unit (PSU)
The power supply unit converts AC mains power into the DC voltages required by the server’s components. Servers often have redundant PSUs, so if one fails, the other can keep the system running.

Network Interface Card (NIC)
The NIC (Network Interface Card) allows the server to connect to a network. It provides one or more network ports with different speeds (for example, 1 Gbit or 10 Gbit) and is often used in multiples for redundancy and higher throughput.
Cooling System (Fans and Heat Sinks)
The cooling system, made up of fans and heat sinks, removes heat generated by components such as the CPU, RAM, and storage. It keeps temperatures within safe limits so the server can operate reliably.

Expansion Slots (PCIe)
PCIe expansion slots are connectors on the motherboard where additional cards can be installed, such as extra network cards, GPUs, or RAID controllers, to expand the server’s capabilities.

Chassis (Rack/Tower/Blade)
The chassis is the physical enclosure of the server.
A tower chassis looks like a desktop PC case.
A rack-mount chassis is designed to be mounted in a data center rack. 
A blade is an extremely compact type of server designed to be used inside a larger shared enclosure. A single blade server does not include its own power supply, cooling system, or some management components. Instead, multiple blades slide into a shared chassis (the blade enclosure), which provides power, cooling, networking, and management for all blades.
In simple terms, a blade is a thin server that relies on a shared enclosure and allows many servers to fit into a very small space.

BIOS/UEFI Firmware
BIOS or UEFI is low-level firmware that runs when the server powers on. It initializes and tests the hardware, then hands control over to the bootloader and operating system. It also stores configuration settings such as boot order and some hardware options.

Backplane
A backplane is a circuit board located behind the drive bays in a server. When you insert a hard drive or SSD into the server, it connects directly to the backplane without any individual data or power cables.
A backplane is a board behind the drive bays. When you push a drive into the server, it connects directly to the backplane without any cables. The backplane provides power and data connections for all drives and sends everything to the RAID controller or motherboard.

2. What are IPMI and iLO, and what are their functions? 
IPMI  “Intelligent Platform Management Interface”
IPMI is a standard interface for out-of-band management of servers. It allows administrators to power the server on or off, monitor temperatures and fan speeds, view hardware logs, and access the remote console even when the operating system is not running.
iLO “Integrated Lights-Out”
iLO is HPE’s proprietary implementation of remote management. It provides similar functionality to IPMI but with a more advanced interface and additional features such as remote console access, virtual media mounting, and detailed hardware monitoring. It is specifically used on HPE servers

3. How do IPMI or iLO relate to the BIOS or UEFI firmware? 
IPMI and iLO run on a separate management controller (BMC: Baseboard Management Controller) that operates independently from the main CPU and the operating system.
They do not replace or integrate into the BIOS/UEFI firmware, but they can interact with it.Because they work at a hardware level, IPMI and iLO allow administrators to:
•	Access the BIOS/UEFI setup remotely
•	View the boot process
•	Change firmware settings
•	Control the system even if the OS is not running or the server has crashed
In summary, IPMI and iLO provide remote, low-level access around the BIOS/UEFI, enabling full control of the server during the boot stage.

4. What are CPU sockets on a server, and what is their purpose? 
A CPU socket is the physical connector on the motherboard where a processor is installed.
Each socket supports specific CPU families, so the type of socket determines which processors a server can use.
Servers may have one or multiple CPU sockets.
Each additional socket allows another physical CPU to be added, increasing the system’s overall processing power and scalability.

5. Why was the pseudo file system introduced in Linux? 
•	Hint: Consider the Linux design philosophy  
Linux introduced pseudo file systems—such as /proc and /sys—to follow the design principle that “everything is a file.”
These file systems do not store data on disk; instead, they provide dynamic, real-time information from the kernel and hardware.
They allow administrators to:
•	View system and kernel data with simple commands
•	Modify certain kernel parameters easily
•	Automate system monitoring and configuration
•	Access kernel information without needing specialized tools

6. What are the differences between a pseudo file system and a normal file system? 
A normal file system, such as ext4 or xfs, is stored on physical disk and contains persistent data like files, directories, and applications. Its contents remain after reboot and follow an on-disk structure.
A pseudo file system, such as /proc or /sys, does not exist on disk. It is created dynamically by the Linux kernel at runtime and exposes live information about kernel state, hardware, and system configuration.
Its contents are not persistent and disappear after reboot.

7. What kind of information is available in the /sys/ directory? 
The /sys directory provides structured, real-time information about the system’s hardware and various kernel subsystems.
It is a pseudo file system generated dynamically by the Linux kernel.
Inside /sys, you can find:
•	Device information (CPU, USB, PCI, block devices, network interfaces)
•	Driver mappings showing which drivers control which devices
•	Kernel subsystem settings (power management, I/O scheduler, modules, etc.)
•	Hardware attributes (fan speeds, CPU frequency, device states)
•	Configuration interfaces that allow modifying kernel behavior by writing to files

8. What is DMA (Direct Memory Access), and what is its use case in Linux? 
DMA (Direct Memory Access) is a hardware feature that allows devices—such as disk controllers, network cards, and sound cards—to transfer data directly to or from memory without involving the CPU.
Without DMA, the CPU would need to handle every data transfer, which would slow down the system and waste processing power.
Use cases in Linux include:
•	Disk read/write operations
•	Network packet transmission and reception
•	GPU and audio data transfers
•	USB device communication
•	Any high-speed I/O operation

9. What does the lsblk command do internally when executed in Linux? 
○ Do lsusb, lspci, and lshw function similarly? 
lsblk:
When lsblk is executed, it does not perform any hardware scanning.
Instead, it reads device information directly from the kernel-exposed pseudo filesystems:
/sys/block
/sys/class/block
These directories contain real-time data about block devices and are dynamically created by the Linux kernel.
lsblk reads the structural information from sysfs and uses udev metadata to display additional device attributes.
lsusb:
lsusb works in a similar way but focuses on USB devices.
It retrieves information from:
/sys/bus/usb/
The USB ID database located at /usr/share/hwdata/usb.ids
(used to map vendor and product IDs to human-readable names)
lspci:
lspci also relies on the sysfs tree, reading PCI device information from:
/sys/bus/pci/
The PCI ID database /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids
This allows it to decode PCI vendor and device identifiers.
lshw:
lshw gathers a broader and more detailed set of hardware information.
It collects data from multiple sources, including:
/sys
/proc
Device drivers
Firmware tables
It uses this data to build a structured, tree-like overview of the system’s hardware.

10. How can we simulate a shutdown operation via the /sys file system? 
/sys/kernel/power/state contains the power modes supported by the system, such as mem (suspend), disk (hibernate), and on some systems off (power off). When we write one of these values to the file — for example:
echo mem > /sys/kernel/power/state
the kernel immediately switches the system into that power state. This mechanism bypasses user-space tools like shutdown and allows us to simulate shutdown-like behavior directly through the kernel’s power-control interface.

11. What are the different types of kernels? 
○ Monolithic vs. Microkernel vs. Hybrid 
○ What are the advantages and disadvantages of each? 
Monolithic Kernel
Most OS services (drivers, file systems, memory, networking) run in kernel space.
Advantages: high performance, fast internal communication.
Disadvantages: a bug can crash the whole system; large and complex.

Microkernel
Only essential functions run in kernel space; drivers and services run in user space.
Advantages: very stable, more secure, modular.
Disadvantages: slower due to IPC; more difficult to implement.

Hybrid Kernel
Mix of monolithic and microkernel ideas; some services run in kernel space for performance.
Advantages: balanced speed and stability; flexible.
Disadvantages: more complex; not fully modular; may inherit monolithic weaknesses.

12. Why is the first sector of a disk used for the MBR? 

The first sector of a disk is used for the MBR because the traditional BIOS firmware always reads this sector first when booting a system.
The BIOS expects the initial bootloader code and the partition table to be located in the first 512 bytes of the disk.
Placing the MBR in the first sector provides:
A fixed, well-defined location for the boot process
Compatibility across all BIOS-based systems
A simple and consistent way for firmware to locate the bootloader
For historical and architectural reasons, this sector became the standard entry point for disk booting.

13. If the MBR is located in the first 512 bytes, how does it know the location of GRUB or another bootloader to load the kernel? 
The MBR is only 512 bytes and can store only a tiny first-stage loader. This loader reads the partition table to find the start of the boot partition and then loads the next-stage bootloader (such as GRUB Stage 2) from that location. This is how the MBR knows where the full bootloader is.

14. What are .efi files, and what is their role in the boot process? 
EFI files are small executable programs that run inside the UEFI firmware. They are stored on the ESP (EFI System Partition) and are loaded directly by UEFI when the computer starts. An .efi file begins the boot process: it prepares the UEFI environment and then loads the full bootloader, such as GRUB. After that, GRUB loads the Linux kernel. In short, .efi files are the programs UEFI uses to start the operating system.

15. What is the ESP (EFI System Partition) in UEFI, and how is it used? 
The ESP (EFI System Partition) is a special FAT32 partition that stores all the files needed for UEFI booting. It contains EFI programs like bootx64.efi and grubx64.efi, as well as bootloader configs and Secure Boot data. When the system starts, UEFI reads this partition and runs an .efi file from it. That file then loads GRUB, and GRUB loads the Linux kernel. Without the ESP, a UEFI system cannot boot.

16. Please explain the following section from /etc/grub/grub.conf: 
1) menuentry 'Ubuntu'
This defines one boot option in the GRUB menu with the name Ubuntu.
2) --class ubuntu, --class gnu-linux, --class gnu, --class os
These class tags help GRUB apply themes, icons, and OS-specific styling.
3) recordfail
GRUB uses this to detect if the previous boot failed, so it can show recovery options.
4) load_video and set gfxmode=auto
These enable graphical mode in GRUB and set the screen resolution automatically.
5) insmod gzio, insmod xzio, insmod lzopio
These commands load GRUB modules that allow reading compressed kernel images.
6) insmod part_gpt and insmod ext2
GRUB loads these modules to understand:
GPT partition tables
ext2/ext4 filesystems
7) set root='hd0,gpt2'
This tells GRUB that the boot partition is on disk 0, GPT partition 2.
8) search --fs-uuid --no-floppy <UUID> --set=root
GRUB finds the correct partition by its UUID, ensuring it boots from the right filesystem.
9) linux /vmlinuz-... root=/dev/mapper/vg0-root ro
This loads the Linux kernel file and tells it:
root filesystem = /dev/mapper/vg0-root
mount as read-only at first (ro)
10) initrd /initrd.img-...
Loads the initramfs, which contains drivers and tools the kernel needs during early boot.
11) }
Marks the end of this menu entry.

This GRUB entry loads the required modules, selects the boot partition, loads the Linux kernel and initrd, and starts the operating system.






